WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.937
NARRATOR: In the lungs, inhaled air reaches
tiny alveoli beside pulmonary capillaries.

2
00:00:05.937 --> 00:00:10.024
NARRATOR: Oxygen crosses the thin respiratory
membrane because its partial pressure is

3
00:00:10.024 --> 00:00:13.090
higher in alveolar air than in the arriving
blood.

4
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.650
NARRATOR: Diffusion continues toward
equilibrium; the membrane does not actively

5
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.930
pump oxygen into the bloodstream.

6
00:00:20.042 --> 00:00:23.510
NARRATOR: Only a small amount of oxygen
remains dissolved in plasma.

7
00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:27.596
NARRATOR: Most enters red blood cells and
binds reversibly to hemoglobin.

8
00:00:27.596 --> 00:00:32.711
NARRATOR: This transport system greatly
increases how much oxygen the blood can carry.

9
00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:37.519
NARRATOR: Oxygenated blood then returns to the
heart and is pumped through the systemic

10
00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:37.862
circulation.

11
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:42.957
NARRATOR: Active tissues consume oxygen during
cellular respiration, lowering oxygen partial

12
00:00:42.957 --> 00:00:44.771
pressure around their cells.

13
00:00:44.771 --> 00:00:49.929
NARRATOR: That gradient favors oxygen leaving
the blood, separating from hemoglobin, and

14
00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:51.218
diffusing into tissue.

15
00:00:51.218 --> 00:00:56.457
NARRATOR: The complete path is driven by
linked gradients: ventilation refreshes

16
00:00:56.457 --> 00:01:00.744
alveolar air, circulation moves blood, and
metabolism maintains demand.
